  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce detailed technical tutorial. Need final only. Need ensure complete sentences, avoid repetition. Need cover parsing, planning, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows, examples tradeoffs pitfalls.\n\nPlan structure:\n- Intro: SQL query execution stages.\n- Example database schema and query. Use concrete: employees, departments, orders? Let's pick: employees(id PK, department_id FK, name, salary), departments(id PK, name), salary_band? Query: SELECT e.name, d.name, e.salary FROM employees e JOIN departments d ON e.department_id = d.id WHERE e.salary > 100000 AND d.name = 'Engineering' ORDER BY e.salary DESC LIMIT 10;\nNeed explain:\n1 Parsing and validation: lexer, parser to AST, semantic analysis, privileges.\n2 Query rewrite/simplification: constant folding, predicate pushdown, common table expressions, view expansion.\n3 Statistics and optimizer: cost-based, cardinality estimates, histograms, selectivity. Plan generation: access paths, join orders, join algorithms. Cost model.\n4 Logical plan: scan, filter, join, aggregate, sort. Physical plan: choose index, nested loop, hash join, merge join.\n5 Execution engine: volcano/object relational, pipelined, operator nodes. Use example plan.\n6 Indexes: B-tree, hash, covering, composite, partial/functional, multi-column order. Example index for query. Tradeoffs: write amplification, storage, maintenance, optimizer misuse.\n7 Joins: nested loop, hash, merge; choice based on cardinality, sortedness, memory. Joins to dimension vs fact. Multi-join order, dynamic filters, runtime filters. Tradeoffs.\n8 Transactions and isolation: concurrency, MVCC, locks, snapshots, ACID. Query execution with read view, index snapshots, consistency, locking on writes. Isolation levels. Tradeoffs. Maybe mention how SELECT may read committed/snapshot; locking reads.\n9 Returning rows: result set streaming vs materialization, network fetch, client cursor, paging, LIMIT early exit.\n10 Pitfalls: missing/wrong stats, selectivity under/over, index on low-card",
